Follow these steps in Ubuntu to install NVM + Node.js + npm.

## Step 1: Open Terminal

Press `Ctrl + Alt + T`.

## Step 2: Install curl and Git

Copy and run:

bash
```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install curl git -y
```

Enter your Ubuntu password if prompted.

## Step 3: Install NVM

Run the official NVM installer for version `0.40.8`:

bash
```
curl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.40.8/install.sh | bash
```

## Step 4: Activate NVM

bash
```
source ~/.bashrc
```

Verify the installation:

bash
```
nvm --version
```

## Step 5: Install Node.js and npm

Install the latest Long-Term Support (LTS) version:

bash
```
nvm install --lts
```

Set it as your default version:

bash
```
nvm alias default 'lts/*'
```

## Step 6: Verify everything

Run these commands separately:

bash
```
node -v
npm -v
nvm --version
```

You should see version numbers for all three.

## Step 7: Test npm

Create a test project:

bash
```
mkdir my-node-app
cd my-node-app
npm init -y
```

This creates a `package.json` file for your project.

Installation complete! You now have NVM, Node.js, and npm installed on Ubuntu. You can use Terminal or the built-in terminal in Visual Studio Code to run Node.js projects.
